course: dictionary
lesson: 6275
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: The word monkey can be a noun or a verb, and it can mean different things. Context tells you which meaning is right. In «In the monkey house a spider monkey hangs by its tail alone», monkey is a real animal. Ask yourself: does this sentence fit a creature in a zoo or a forest?
explain: Read this: «My little brother is a real monkey, he hid my shoes again.» A monkey in a tree would not hide shoes, so monkey here means a cheeky child who plays tricks. Try your meaning in the sentence and check that it still makes sense.
explain: Words in phrases change meaning too. «Monkey bars» are climbing frames in the playground, and «monkey around» means to play about in a silly way. When monkey sits next to another word, look at the phrase as a whole. On the monkey bars, hold on with both hands and go one child at a time.
explain: Look at «Vervet monkeys have different alarm calls for leopards, eagles and snakes». The context tells you these monkeys are animals that warn each other about danger. Use the clues first, and only then check a dictionary if you are still unsure.
fact: Monkey can be a noun (a monkey, monkeys) or a verb (monkey around).
fact: «Monkey» means a clever animal with long arms and legs, hands that grip, and usually a long tail, that lives mostly in trees in hot countries.
fact: «Monkey» can also mean a cheeky child who plays tricks; this is informal, so use it with friends, not with teachers.
fact: «Monkey around» means to play about in a silly way, and «monkey business» means naughty behaviour.
fact: Monkeys are mammals and primates, like apes and humans; most monkeys have tails, but apes such as gorillas and chimpanzees do not.
exercise: «In the monkey house a spider monkey hangs by its tail alone like a fifth hand.» What is a monkey in this sentence? | A kind of nut | * A clever animal with long arms and legs | A cheeky child who plays tricks | A climbing frame in the playground
exercise: «My little brother is a real monkey, he hid my shoes again.» What does monkey mean here? | A tall tree in the forest | To play about in a silly way | A wild animal in a zoo | * A cheeky child who plays tricks
exercise: Ava says, «Stop trying to monkey around and sit down!» What does monkey around mean? | To climb the monkey bars | To eat nuts with stones | To look after a monkey | * To play about in a silly way
test: In «Wild capuchin monkeys in Brazil crack hard nuts with stones», what do the monkeys use to crack the nuts? | Their teeth alone | Metal spoons | Their long tails | * Stones
test: What does «hundreds of kinds of monkeys» tell us? | * There are many different types of monkey | Monkeys only live in one city | Only one kind of monkey exists | Monkeys are very rare
exam: Dani says «monkey» always means an animal, so «monkey business» must be about animals too. Is Dani right? | Yes, a monkey business is a zoo job | No, in «monkey business» it means naughty behaviour | Yes, because «monkey» is always a noun | * No, since «monkey» can also mean a cheeky child, and the phrase means naughty behaviour
